  "headline": "Relief, not a verdict: I keep the 10-year, add gold and concede the dollar is winning",
  "body": [
    "Today is a relief rally, not a verdict. Stocks bounced 4.7% and the VIX fell to 26.6, well off the top of its range, while gold rose 3.2% on the same day the dollar made a 52-week high. A metal rallying against a strong dollar is the tell I care about, and it showed up. But the 5-year sold off 3 basis points while the 30-year rallied 1: the cut panic is being partly unwound and nobody is dumping the long end.",
    "Friday's read held where it mattered. The 10-year is unchanged at 4.92%, inside my 5.15% line, and gold has gained since my first buy at $3,539. What I got wrong is the character of the gold bid: it came on a collapsing VIX, so it is relief buying rather than fear. Over the week the front end still led, down 21 basis points against 17 for the 30-year, so the direction stands.",
    "The dollar is winning this quarter and I will say it plainly. The index is at the top of its year's range, up 5.4% over three months, while gold is down 22% over the same span and sits near its 52-week low. That is the opposite of my tell. I read it as a timing problem, a funding squeeze running ahead of the slow tide, because the 30-year minus 5-year spread has still widened 6 basis points over the month. If the dollar keeps printing highs with gold under $3,450 and the long end rallying harder than the front, the thesis is wrong for this cycle.",
    "Oil is the missing accelerant. Brent is down 20% over the month to $89, so the market is pricing no supply shock, and the rupee has strengthened 2.4% over the month: importers are being paid, not punished. Bitcoin managed 0.6% on a day stocks rose 4.7%, so it could not even rally as a risk asset; it buys 16.6 ounces of gold, down 17.5% over the month, and that ratio has not turned.",
    "I keep the 10-year and the dollar, and I add to gold because it rose with the dollar at a high. I stay out of bitcoin, Brent and copper, and I still do not own the 30-year at 5.29%. I am wrong on the 10-year above 5.15% with stocks falling, and wrong on gold below $3,450. MOVE at 101 is still three-quarters of the way up its range, so one good day has not calmed the bond market; I watch it and the 30-year minus 5-year spread, which needs to resume toward 70 basis points."
  ],
